WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.989
<font color="#00ff00"> Fixed & Synced by : Abdalhmohmd </font> 

2
00:00:32.366 --> 00:00:35.267
My first
visual memories are of this

3
00:00:35.302 --> 00:00:38.770
framed poster above my crib.

4
00:00:43.577 --> 00:00:46.545
I would stare at it every
night before I went to bed.

5
00:00:51.285 --> 00:00:54.519
My father was an underground
comic distributor.

6
00:00:54.555 --> 00:00:57.489
He's always been this sort of
Zelig of the counterculture

7
00:00:57.524 --> 00:00:59.457
as I like to call him.

8
00:00:59.493 --> 00:01:02.761
Having painted in
Warhol's studio,

9
00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:06.865
being roomies with Lou
Reed, he knew R. Crumb

10
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.268
and all these great
artists of his generation.

11
00:01:10.304 --> 00:01:14.372
I mean, I was brought up on all
kinds of wacky visuals as a kid.

12
00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:20.745
So I would stare at this
painting over and over:

13
00:01:20.781 --> 00:01:25.216
The Garden of Earthly Delights
by Hieronymus Bosch.

14
00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:32.223
It was painted around 1500.

15
00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:34.793
And if you look at these
panels long enough,

16
00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:36.761
they start to tell a story.

17
00:01:39.533 --> 00:01:43.702
In the first panel you have Adam
and Eve in the Garden of Eden.

18
00:01:43.737 --> 00:01:46.271
Birds flying off
into the distance,

19
00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:51.142
elephants and giraffe and a
lot of religious iconography.

20
00:01:51.178 --> 00:01:53.612
The second panel is where
it starts to become

21
00:01:53.647 --> 00:01:56.414
more interesting.

22
00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:59.551
The deadly sins start to infuse
their way into the painting.

23
00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:03.355
There's overpopulation,
there's debauchery and excess.

24
00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.462
And the last panel, which
is the most nightmarish one,

25
00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:16.701
especially from a young child's
perspective, is this twisted,

26
00:02:16.737 --> 00:02:20.405
decayed, burnt landscape.

27
00:02:21.475 --> 00:02:25.677
A paradise that has been
degraded and destroyed.

28
00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:33.852
Man may be unwittingly changing the world's

29
00:02:33.887 --> 00:02:37.455
climate through the waste
products of its civilization...

30
00:02:37.491 --> 00:02:41.126
The burning of coal, oil and
wood is releasing carbon dioxide

31
00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:43.228
into the atmosphere.

32
00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:46.598
The greenhouse effect has been
detected and it is changing

33
00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:48.400
our climate now.

34
00:02:48.435 --> 00:02:50.902
Guess where the
temperature goes?

35
00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:53.571
It goes up.

36
00:02:53.607 --> 00:02:55.640
Climate change is
already in our backyards.

37
00:02:55.676 --> 00:02:57.642
It's official. 2012 was the
hottest year in the...

38
00:02:57.678 --> 00:02:58.743
...2013

39
00:02:58.779 --> 00:03:00.145
...2014

40
00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.246
...2015

41
00:03:01.281 --> 00:03:02.514
...warmest year around the globe

42
00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:03.748
...warmest year on record.

43
00:03:03.784 --> 00:03:06.851
It is these activities
that drove mankind

44
00:03:06.887 --> 00:03:08.386
out of the Garden of Eden.

45
00:03:16.897 --> 00:03:18.630
Hi, how are you?
Ah, Mr. DiCaprio.

46
00:03:18.665 --> 00:03:19.664
Pleasure, pleasure.

47
00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.467
Great to, great
pleasure to see you.

48
00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:24.369
Here.

49
00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:26.171
We can move this.

50
00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:28.139
This can be adjusted.

51
00:03:28.175 --> 00:03:29.207
Oh wow, this
is for height control.

52
00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:30.608
Yeah.

53
00:03:30.644 --> 00:03:32.310
Like this, like this.
For shorter leaders

54
00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:34.646
and taller leaders.

55
00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:37.682
What specific message do you
think is the most important?

56
00:03:37.718 --> 00:03:41.286
Climate change is
coming much, much faster.

57
00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:46.124
We have seen such, uh,
extraordinary extreme weather

58
00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:47.826
patterns.

59
00:03:47.861 --> 00:03:51.463
If you consider this
vastness of this universe,

60
00:03:51.498 --> 00:03:55.133
this Planet Earth is
just a small boat.

61
00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:59.637
If this boat is sinking, then
I think we will have to all

62
00:03:59.673 --> 00:04:01.239
sink together.

63
00:04:02.776 --> 00:04:06.644
I know that he has been a
very committed and effective

64
00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:11.583
environmentalist and that is
why I really count on your

65
00:04:11.618 --> 00:04:13.718
leadership and vision.

66
00:04:15.422 --> 00:04:18.156
Please welcome
newly appointed United Nations

67
00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:22.393
Messenger of Peace,
Mr. Leonardo DiCaprio.

68
00:04:27.434 --> 00:04:29.567
Thank you
Mr. Secretary General,

69
00:04:29.603 --> 00:04:30.869
Your Excellencies...

70
00:04:30.904 --> 00:04:33.304
I feel incredibly honored
that they chose me to be

71
00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:36.307
the United Nations Messenger
of Peace on climate change.

72
00:04:36.343 --> 00:04:39.577
I stand before
you, not as an expert,

73
00:04:39.613 --> 00:04:43.782
I want to do everything I can
to learn more about this issue.

74
00:04:43.817 --> 00:04:48.787
See for myself exactly what's
going on and what can be done.

75
00:04:48.822 --> 00:04:52.423
But it all kinda seems
beyond our control.

76
00:04:52.459 --> 00:04:55.593
As an actor,
I pretend for a living.

77
00:04:55.629 --> 00:04:58.696
I play fictitious characters
often solving fictitious

78
00:04:58.732 --> 00:05:00.431
problems.

79
00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:03.301
I believe that mankind has
looked at climate change

80
00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:04.836
in that same way.

81
00:05:04.871 --> 00:05:07.338
We keep getting inundated with
catastrophic news about the

82
00:05:07.374 --> 00:05:10.775
environment every single day,
and the problem seems to be

83
00:05:10.811 --> 00:05:15.079
getting worse and
worse and worse.

84
00:05:15.115 --> 00:05:17.849
Try to have a conversation with
anyone about climate change,

85
00:05:17.884 --> 00:05:19.450
people just tune out.

86
00:05:22.289 --> 00:05:26.090
If the U.N. really knew
how I feel,

87
00:05:26.126 --> 00:05:32.730
how pessimistic I am about our
future, I mean, to be honest,

88
00:05:32.766 --> 00:05:35.300
they may have picked
the wrong guy.

89
00:05:37.704 --> 00:05:39.037
Hundreds of
thousands took to the streets

90
00:05:39.072 --> 00:05:44.209
of New York today demonstrating
for action on climate change.

91
00:05:44.244 --> 00:05:46.744
Secretary General,
Ban Ki-Moon called Leo

92
00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.480
a credible voice in the
environmental movement,

93
00:05:49.516 --> 00:05:52.250
which is like calling Jeffrey
Dahmer a credible voice

94
00:05:52.285 --> 00:05:53.818
for stew.

95
00:05:53.854 --> 00:05:57.255
Leonardo DiCaprio
has just naively bought into

96
00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:59.557
the entire global warming hoax.

97
00:05:59.593 --> 00:06:01.593
Who better to
educate world leaders on made up

98
00:06:01.628 --> 00:06:05.196
climate change in a crisis that
doesn't exist than an actor

99
00:06:05.232 --> 00:06:07.832
from Hollywood with zero years
of scientific training?

100
00:06:18.612 --> 00:06:19.444
Hi, how are you?

101
00:06:19.479 --> 00:06:20.678
I'm very good.

102
00:06:20.714 --> 00:06:22.080
How are you doing,
it's a pleasure.

103
00:06:22.115 --> 00:06:22.814
You too.

104
00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:26.851
Hey!

105
00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:32.857
Nice to
see you, buddy.

106
00:06:32.893 --> 00:06:34.192
Good to see you.

107
00:06:34.227 --> 00:06:38.363
So I just started this new film,
it's set in the Western Frontier

108
00:06:38.398 --> 00:06:41.666
of the United States
in the early 1800s.

109
00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:44.569
It's basically the dawn of
the Industrial Revolution.

110
00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:48.106
It has a lot to do with men who
are pillaging the natural world,

111
00:06:48.141 --> 00:06:50.875
wiping species out, and
basically destroying cultures

112
00:06:50.911 --> 00:06:52.210
in the process.

113
00:06:52.245 --> 00:06:54.679
Those are like buffalo skulls.

114
00:06:54.714 --> 00:06:56.114
Alejandro!

115
00:06:56.149 --> 00:06:59.317
Crazy, are these fur-trappers?

116
00:06:59.352 --> 00:07:00.685
Are these people
selling the skulls?

117
00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.288
No, these are people
selling the skulls, yeah.

118
00:07:03.323 --> 00:07:05.123
What the is this?

119
00:07:05.158 --> 00:07:06.357
Isn't that embarrassing?

120
00:07:06.393 --> 00:07:08.059
Man, that's crazy.

121
00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:09.594
And the fact that
they never even reflected on it,

122
00:07:09.629 --> 00:07:12.430
that there's an ending to these
animals, and we cannot just,

123
00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:13.698
it's insane.

124
00:07:13.733 --> 00:07:15.300
If you can put that
in the movie that would be

125
00:07:15.335 --> 00:07:17.502
unbelievable.

126
00:07:17.537 --> 00:07:18.569
How about this, I dare you!

127
00:07:18.605 --> 00:07:21.205
Ah, okay.

128
00:07:21.241 --> 00:07:25.443
...get to the boat. Come on son.

129
00:07:28.581 --> 00:07:30.615
I didn't grow up
around nature at all,

130
00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:35.219
I grew up near
downtown Los Angeles.

131
00:07:35.255 --> 00:07:40.325
And my escape every weekend was
the Natural History Museum.

132
00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:44.228
And from a very young age, I
became fascinated with species

133
00:07:44.264 --> 00:07:46.764
that had become extinct.

134
00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:48.366
All these different animals,

135
00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:50.702
from the dodo to the
passenger pigeon,

136
00:07:50.737 --> 00:07:53.338
which was the most
plentiful bird on earth,

137
00:07:53.373 --> 00:07:56.774
to the Tasmanian tiger, also
known as the thilacene

138
00:07:56.810 --> 00:08:00.278
to the great auk, the
quagga, the moa.

139
00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:05.283
I remember the
anger that I felt,

140
00:08:05.318 --> 00:08:08.586
reading all these stories about
how explorers and settlers

141
00:08:08.621 --> 00:08:13.491
would just wipe out an entire
species, and in the process,

142
00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:16.127
decimate the ecosystem forever.

143
00:08:25.672 --> 00:08:30.174
The difference now is we're
knowingly doing this;

144
00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:35.146
it's just on a
much larger scale.

145
00:08:43.323 --> 00:08:45.790
If we're gonna fight
climate change effectively,

146
00:08:45.825 --> 00:08:47.792
we have to start by
acknowledging that

147
00:08:47.827 --> 00:08:51.262
most of our economy is
based on fossil fuels.

148
00:08:51.297 --> 00:08:56.134
Fossil fuels are coal,
oil, and natural gas.

149
00:08:56.169 --> 00:08:58.336
Oil powers most of the
transportation sector,

150
00:09:01.808 --> 00:09:05.710
coal and natural gas power
most of the electricity.

151
00:09:07.714 --> 00:09:11.315
Now, in the first couple decades
of the twenty-first century,

152
00:09:11.351 --> 00:09:14.819
in order to sustain our
dependence on fossil fuels,

153
00:09:14.854 --> 00:09:18.823
we're going to very risky,
very extreme new sources.

154
00:09:22.195 --> 00:09:25.630
You see this in things like
mountain top removal for coal,

155
00:09:28.468 --> 00:09:30.201
fracking for natural gas,

156
00:09:34.207 --> 00:09:36.374
offshore drilling for oil,

157
00:09:39.245 --> 00:09:43.081
and the tar sands, which is
the most devastating form

158
00:09:43.116 --> 00:09:46.150
of producing fossil fuels.

159
00:09:46.186 --> 00:09:50.321
They take away massive forests,
the water in the streams

160
00:09:50.356 --> 00:09:52.590
and rivers is poisoned.

161
00:09:52.625 --> 00:09:56.727
It has severe impacts on the
wildlife, on native communities,

162
00:09:56.763 --> 00:10:02.433
and it requires a huge amount of
energy simply to get it to

163
00:10:02.469 --> 00:10:03.468
our fuel tanks.

164
00:10:05.405 --> 00:10:07.705
There is no such thing
as clean fossil fuel.

165
00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.340
Headsets are hung up at the back,

166
00:10:09.375 --> 00:10:10.875
but the volume control's right
above the microphone so...

167
00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:53.644
The truth is, the more
I've learned about this issue

168
00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.415
and everything that
contributes to the problem,

169
00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:59.850
the more I realize
how much I don't know.

170
00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:02.687
How much I don't know
about this issue.

171
00:12:05.158 --> 00:12:08.292
The first time I ever heard
the term global warming,

172
00:12:08.328 --> 00:12:10.127
which was what it
was called back then,

173
00:12:10.163 --> 00:12:12.230
I was in my early twenties.

174
00:12:12.265 --> 00:12:14.832
I had the opportunity to sit
down one on one with then

175
00:12:14.867 --> 00:12:18.469
Vice President, Al Gore
in the White House.

176
00:12:18.504 --> 00:12:21.572
And he drew a picture of
our planet on a chalkboard,

177
00:12:21.608 --> 00:12:24.141
and then he drew the atmosphere
around it, and he said,

178
00:12:24.177 --> 00:12:27.712
this is the most important
issue of our time.

179
00:12:27.747 --> 00:12:31.782
I had absolutely no idea
what he was talking about.

180
00:12:31.818 --> 00:12:35.219
He said all our modes of
transportation, boats, trains,

181
00:12:35.255 --> 00:12:37.755
planes, cars, the way
we produce our food,

182
00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:40.524
the way we build our cities,
almost everything we do releases

183
00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:46.731
carbon dioxide, CO2, and that
leads to climate change.

184
00:12:48.635 --> 00:12:51.702
The polar ice caps will melt,
the seas will start to rise,

185
00:12:51.738 --> 00:12:54.639
there will be more dangerous
weather patterns, floods,

186
00:12:54.674 --> 00:12:56.607
droughts, wildfires.

187
00:12:56.643 --> 00:12:59.510
It sounded like some nightmarish
science fiction film.

188
00:13:01.748 --> 00:13:03.848
Except everything
he said is real

189
00:13:03.883 --> 00:13:05.549
and it's happening right now.

190
00:13:12.825 --> 00:13:14.425
Wow.

191
00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:15.293
Wow.

192
00:13:15.328 --> 00:13:16.360
Beautiful.

193
00:13:21.567 --> 00:13:25.169
I just want to know
how far we've gone,

194
00:13:25.204 --> 00:13:28.839
how much damage we've done, and
if there's anything we can do

195
00:13:28.875 --> 00:13:30.908
to stop it.

196
00:13:36.282 --> 00:13:36.747
You all set, mate? !

197
00:13:41.387 --> 00:13:43.220
So it's the 4th of July,

198
00:13:43.256 --> 00:13:45.456
and we're here in the
middle of the Arctic.

199
00:13:45.491 --> 00:13:48.225
Right now we are
standing on the ocean,

200
00:13:48.261 --> 00:13:50.561
we are basically
walking on water.

201
00:13:50.596 --> 00:13:53.230
This is the northern
tip of Baffin Island,

202
00:13:53.266 --> 00:13:56.400
one of the hundreds of islands
in the High Canadian Arctic.

203
00:13:56.436 --> 00:13:59.203
We are way above
the Arctic Circle,

204
00:13:59.238 --> 00:14:01.172
and if you put your
hand in the water,

205
00:14:01.207 --> 00:14:03.774
you would not be able to stand
it for more than 5 minutes,

206
00:14:06.379 --> 00:14:09.080
except you who are a badass.

207
00:14:09.115 --> 00:14:10.247
You hunt and fish here,
right?

208
00:14:10.283 --> 00:14:12.049
Yeah.

209
00:14:12.085 --> 00:14:13.784
What do you, what do you fish
and what do you hunt?

210
00:14:13.820 --> 00:14:18.456
In my territory I hunt fish,
seal, narwhal,

211
00:14:18.491 --> 00:14:20.024
and polar bear.

212
00:14:20.059 --> 00:14:21.192
Is this polar bear?
Yup.

213
00:14:21.227 --> 00:14:23.294
Oh, well there you go!

214
00:14:23.329 --> 00:14:26.831
And the ice here, since you've
been around has it been

215
00:14:26.866 --> 00:14:27.798
decreasing?

216
00:14:27.834 --> 00:14:31.035
We used to have
a solid ice, blue.

217
00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:33.070
Not light blue like this, blue.

218
00:14:33.106 --> 00:14:34.839
Hard, ice.

219
00:14:34.874 --> 00:14:42.546
Now we usually have a, kind
of ice cream type of ice.

220
00:14:42.582 --> 00:14:45.015
It's ice but it's,
when it starts to melt,

221
00:14:45.051 --> 00:14:46.917
it melts pretty fast.

222
00:14:46.953 --> 00:14:49.153
Much faster than before.

223
00:14:49.188 --> 00:14:54.959
2040, you will be able
to sail over the North Pole.

224
00:14:54.994 --> 00:14:58.028
There's going to be no sea
ice left in the Arctic Ocean

225
00:14:58.064 --> 00:14:59.830
in the summer.

226
00:14:59.866 --> 00:15:03.200
We are burning so many fossil
fuels that the ice is melting.

227
00:15:06.205 --> 00:15:09.240
The Arctic is like
the air conditioning for

228
00:15:09.275 --> 00:15:11.342
the Northern Hemisphere.

229
00:15:11.377 --> 00:15:14.445
If it goes away, that's
going to change currents,

230
00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:17.014
that's going to change
weather patterns,

231
00:15:17.049 --> 00:15:21.819
that's going to make floods and
droughts more catastrophic.

232
00:15:21.854 --> 00:15:25.489
It's the most dramatic
transformation of a large

233
00:15:25.525 --> 00:15:27.024
environment ever.

234
00:15:30.963 --> 00:15:31.429
Look at this.

235
00:15:34.367 --> 00:15:37.334
They're right here!

236
00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:40.438
There are about 10,000
to 12,000 narwhals

237
00:15:40.473 --> 00:15:42.773
in this region.

238
00:15:42.809 --> 00:15:46.343
The numbers are stable here,
in other regions are declining.

239
00:15:46.379 --> 00:15:48.479
Wow.

240
00:15:48.514 --> 00:15:50.414
That's awesome.

241
00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:53.951
Yeah, they are waiting
for the ice to open up,

242
00:15:53.986 --> 00:15:56.987
to swim in, so they
can catch the flounder.

243
00:15:57.023 --> 00:16:02.059
I can't believe what they sound like,
it's amazing.

244
00:16:02.094 --> 00:16:02.993
They're like purring.

245
00:16:06.466 --> 00:16:08.098
You know, I don't want
to be in a planet

246
00:16:08.134 --> 00:16:09.467
without these animals.

247
00:16:17.543 --> 00:16:19.276
He's the chairman of Earth Day 2000,

248
00:16:19.312 --> 00:16:20.778
Leonardo DiCaprio.

249
00:16:20.813 --> 00:16:22.980
When I was 25 years old,

250
00:16:23.015 --> 00:16:26.083
I remember being asked to
participate in this huge event

251
00:16:26.118 --> 00:16:27.885
in Washington for Earth Day.

